 11698                                  .rtks_wait:
 11699                                      ; Force-flush any posted DMA write the NIC has queued upstream before
 11700                                      ; trusting the in-RAM copy of the descriptor. Two separate runs of this
 11701                                      ; exact failure now show the identical, deterministic pattern: OWN and
 11702                                      ; the length field both read back cleared, TOK never observed set
 11703                                      ; during the whole polling window, and yet no PCI abort/parity fault -
 11704                                      ; and critically, the descriptor only ever shows the "completed" state
 11705                                      ; *after* netdiag_dump_tx's own PCI config-space read runs, never while
 11706                                      ; this loop is plain-RAM-polling. PCI/PCIe ordering only guarantees a
 11707                                      ; posted write has landed once a read that traverses the same path
 11708                                      ; completes ("reads push writes") - a legacy CF8/CFC config cycle is a
 11709                                      ; much stronger/slower completion barrier on real chipsets than an I/O
 11710                                      ; BAR register read, and is exactly what netdiag_dump_tx was doing
 11711                                      ; right before the "already done" readback. So issue that same kind of
 11712                                      ; read ourselves, every iteration, instead of only reading raw system
 11713                                      ; RAM and hoping the write has already arrived.
